        <groundspeak:log id="249661496">
          <groundspeak:date>2012-07-15T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="1565005">Takin it Easy</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP551 Park Court

Posted: N53° 43.086 W114° 53.657

GPSr: N53° 43.085 W114° 53.661&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This was the last brass cap on our trip to Hay River and back.  We visited 48 cap locations.  We had finds on 42, 3 we could not find anything, 1 was just a capless post and was recommended for archival, 1 was under an active lookout tower and recommended for archival and 1 we were told by CNR police to leave the area as we were trespassing.  We spotted the supposed world's largest grizzly, beaver, moose, teepee, mosquito, walleye, sun dial, bee, dragon fly, wagon wheel, pick, pumpkin patch, sausage, mallard and mushroom as well as Henry 12 foot Fuller Davis, Depression Pete and a combine weathervane.  We also found a few caches during the 5000 km trip.  The only hiccup was a 7 hour wait while a massive fire burned across the highway across from us and a few extremely smoky towns.  A great and memorable adventure.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Park Court is a fine rural location about 1km down a gravel road off the highway north.  We found convenient parking and quickly spied the cap and took the required photos.  On the way back we visited Wabamaun Lake Prov Park and found a few caches.  Hwy 16 was closed due to a police emergency causing us to take northern secondary roads (16A was a parking lot) east to get around the closure, but we made it back for the last track race just in time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for keeping this virtual going OFTH!  It is by far our favourite caching activity and we greatly appreciate your efforts on it!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This entry was edited by Takin it Easy on Tuesday, 17 July 2012 at 00:36:10 UTC.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
